  "narrative": "The new panel separates into one large new association, several strong robustness checks for prior accepted findings, and several near-null or opposite-direction effects. Because returned estimates are signed to each hypothesis direction, a positive signed estimate supports the registered claim. H15 is the key new discovery: TP53 loss versus TP53-intact screens for dependency_USP7 with direction +1 has signed estimate +0.396, 95% interval +0.325 to +0.467, n=406 vs 1594. Since direction +1 corresponds to a positive raw exposed-minus-comparator difference, TP53-loss cells show much weaker USP7 dependency (less negative scores) than TP53-intact cells. This is far above a 10% scale on the USP7 dependency distribution and is precise, so I accept H15 as a dataset-level association and request independent validation as the only voluntary validation this iteration. I did not validate H6 or H20 immediately despite their accepted status because their new subset analyses H21/H22/H25/H26 are highly concordant; H15 is a newly accepted claim with no independent confirmation yet and is therefore the higher-priority validation target. The MYC amplification panel is uninformative for the registered negative-direction hypotheses: H17 POLR2A signed estimate +0.004 with interval -0.017 to +0.025, H18 RPL11 signed estimate -0.012 with interval -0.034 to +0.009, and H19 DHX9 signed estimate -0.003 with interval -0.024 to +0.019 are all near null, and in each case the interval excludes a clinically meaningful effect in the predicted direction. I reject H17-H19 and close them. H13 NF1 loss/KRAS dependency signed estimate -0.020, interval -0.049 to +0.009, is small and opposite the predicted stronger KRAS dependency; because support is excluded beyond a clinically meaningful positive signed effect, I reject the overall H13 claim, though the already registered KRAS-mutation-free specificity variant H30 can remain for later review. H16 ARID1A loss/SMARCA2 dependency signed estimate -0.017, interval -0.057 to +0.023, does not support stronger SMARCA2 dependency; I reject the original H16 overall but register a refinement in SMARCA4-intact cells to test whether a weaker independent ARID1A effect can be detected once the dominant SMARCA4-loss effect is removed. H14 BRCA2 loss/WRN dependency is essentially null, signed estimate -0.0001, interval -0.045 to +0.045; the interval still includes positive support above the approximate 10% scale, so I defer rather than reject, pending the MSI-intact refinement H29. The robustness checks are strong: H21 MSI-high/WRN within Avana signed +0.794 interval +0.764 to +0.825; H22 within TP53-intact signed +0.780 interval +0.747 to +0.813; H24 SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 within Avana signed +0.781 interval +0.760 to +0.802; H25 KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 within TP53-intact signed +0.792 interval +0.770 to +0.815; H26 within MSI-intact signed +0.798 interval +0.777 to +0.819. These support H6, H8, and H20 without independent validation, but I still treat them as associations within this supplied dataset and not as causal or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Accepted dataset-level claims now include H6 active (supported by H21/H22), H8 closed after validation (supported by H24), H20 active (supported by H25/H26), and newly accepted H15 active. H15: TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency in this dataset; validation H15 requested. Rejected/closed: H13, H16, H17, H18, H19, plus earlier H3/H9/H10/H12. Deferred: H14, H2, H5, H7, H11, H23, H27-H30 pending value. New proposals registered: H15 restricted to Avana library for technical robustness, and ARID1A loss on SMARCA2 restricted to SMARCA4-intact screens to test an independent SWI/SNF effect without dilution from SMARCA4 loss. Next synthesize/validation stage: assess H15 validation immediately; if validated, keep H15 strong and consider Avana specificity, if invalidated downgrade/reopen. If H6/H20 remain central and later validation capacity exists, validate them after H15. Do not claim causality or generalizability; all findings are dataset associations from measured predictors and dependency scores."
}

