  "narrative": "H102 response deadline assessed: the PTEN-loss versus non-PTEN-loss contrast for dependency_STAT3 among TP53-loss cells is clinically meaningful and independently supported. Discovery R99 gave a signed mean difference of +0.190 with 95% interval +0.085 to +0.296, and independent validation R179 gave +0.298 with an independent interval +0.141 to +0.454. Because H102 has direction -1, positive signed estimates correspond to more negative dependency_STAT3 in PTEN-loss cells, i.e. stronger STAT3 dependency, within the TP53-loss eligible subgroup. The validated lower bound is above the +0.10 clinically meaningful benchmark, so I accept H102 and keep the investigation closed. This strengthens the central PTEN-loss/STAT3 association in a TP53-loss context, but it remains a within-dataset association and not a causal claim.\n\nFinal scientific report: The dataset supports five central measured-predictor associations with scientifically meaningful differences in CRISPR dependency scores. MSI-high cells show much stronger dependency_WRN; SMARCA4-loss cells show much stronger dependency_SMARCA2; KEAP1-loss cells show much weaker dependency_NFE2L2; TP53-loss cells show much weaker dependency_USP7; and PTEN-loss cells show stronger dependency_STAT3. The central effect sizes are very large for MSI-high/WRN, SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2, and KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2, with signed effects near +0.78 to +0.81 and validated intervals far above +0.10. TP53-loss/USP7 is a robust moderate effect near +0.38 to +0.40 with validated support. PTEN-loss/STAT3 is a robust moderate effect near +0.27 to +0.33 with validated support, and the TP53-loss-stratified estimate now has independent validation at +0.298 with lower bound +0.141.\n\nThe central conclusions are not limited to one screen library, lineage subtype, QC subset, batch, mutation-burden band, or PIK3CA stratum. The PTEN-loss/STAT3 effect is clinically benchmarked in PIK3CA-wildtype and PIK3CA-mutant cells, TP53-wildtype and TP53-loss cells, KRAS-wildtype and KRAS-mutant cells, MYC-amplified cells, CDKN2A-loss contexts, KEAP1-loss contexts, and MTAP-loss contexts. The SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 effect remains large across Avana and KY libraries, batch contexts, TP53 and CDKN2A contexts, PIK3CA strata, ARID1A-loss contexts, MYC-stratified checks, and SMARCA4-loss specificity checks. The KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 effect is strong in PIK3CA strata, MYC-amplified cells, TP53-loss and TP53-wildtype cells, ARID1A-loss and ARID1A-wildtype contexts, and MSI-negative cells. The TP53-loss/USP7 effect is robust across PIK3CA strata, CDKN2A contexts, KEAP1 strata, ARID1A contexts, PIK3CA strata, Avana and validated KY contexts, and mutation-burden bands; interaction by mutation burden is not clinically meaningful after validation.\n\nNegative findings are substantial. The data do not support clinically meaningful oncotarget-concordant dependencies for several canonical predictors: KRAS mutation is not meaningfully associated with stronger dependency_KRAS, BRAF V600E is not meaningfully associated with dependency_BRAF, EGFR amplification is not meaningfully associated with dependency_EGFR, and ERBB2 amplification is not meaningfully associated with dependency_ERBB2. MYC amplification does not show a clinically meaningful association with dependency_WRN, dependency_SMARCA2 in SMARCA4-wildtype cells, dependency_USP7, or most other tested dependencies, despite a small direction-consistent MYC/WRN effect below the benchmark. ARID1A loss is not a clinically meaningful independent predictor of dependency_SMARCA2 in SMARCA4-wildtype cells, and STK11 loss is not a meaningful independent predictor of dependency_NFE2L2 in KEAP1-wildtype cells. BRCA2 loss does not show a clinically meaningful association with dependency_WRN in MSI-high-negative cells, supporting specificity of the WRN association for MSI-high status rather than a broader DNA-damage-confounded signal. MTAP loss is not a clinically meaningful independent predictor of dependency_USP7 or dependency_MDM2. Research markers D, E, and F do not produce central clinically meaningful effects on the tested dependency scores.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains. The analysis is association-level within this supplied dataset; it does not establish that these predictor changes cause dependency changes, nor does it establish external generalizability. Some effect-modification questions are not fully settled: interaction intervals for several co-occurring pathway contexts are wide or do not meet the +0.10 threshold, and the KY CRISPR-library PTEN-loss/STAT3 subgroup remains below the clinical benchmark even though the central PTEN-loss/STAT3 effect is accepted and validated. These subgroup-level unresolved items do not weaken the main within-dataset conclusions, but they prevent strong claims of equal effect size across all libraries or co-mutation strata.\n\nMost informative next analysis: additional discovery of main effects is less valuable than targeted replication or multivariable assessment of effect modification among central pathway contexts. If only internal resources are used, the most informative next comparisons would pre-specify and validate interactions such as PTEN loss x TP53 loss for dependency_STAT3 and TP53 loss x KEAP1 loss for dependency_USP7, because the main effects are already robust but their co-occurrence interactions have wide uncertainty. Ideally, the next step would be an independent external validation cohort or an adjusted joint model that tests simultaneous co-mutations, rather than continuing to expand subgroup-specific Welch estimates.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 17 synthesis complete. H102 accepted and closed based on R99 and R179: TP53-loss-stratified PTEN-loss effect on dependency_STAT3 is clinically meaningful and independently validated, signed estimate +0.298 with independent lower bound +0.141. Final within-dataset conclusions remain five accepted associations: MSI-high -> stronger dependency_WRN, SMARCA4 loss -> stronger dependency_SMARCA2, KEAP1 loss -> weaker dependency_NFE2L2, TP53 loss -> weaker dependency_USP7, and PTEN loss -> stronger dependency_STAT3. Robustness checks across PIK3CA, MYC, TP53, KEAP1, ARID1A, CDKN2A, KRAS, lineage, library, batch, QC, and mutation-burden strata support these as stable within-dataset associations rather than one-context artifacts. Negative findings exclude many canonical oncotarget-concordant main effects and broad MYC/ARID1A/STK11/BRCA2/MTAP confounding claims. Remaining uncertainty is about causality, external generalizability, and exact effect modification. Most informative next work is independent external validation or a multivariable/co-mutation interaction analysis, especially PTEN x TP53 for STAT3 and TP53 x KEAP1 for USP7, not more exploratory main-effect subgroups. No further internal validation is required for the central claims in this run."
}

