  "narrative": "No assessments are due. I use this explore step to move from repeated main-effect robustness checks toward pre-specified co-mutation decomposition for the two most informative unresolved pathway interactions: TP53 loss x KEAP1 loss for dependency_USP7 and PTEN loss x TP53 loss for dependency_STAT3. The central associations are already accepted and independently validated: MSI-high with much stronger dependency_WRN, SMARCA4 loss with much stronger dependency_SMARCA2, KEAP1 loss with weaker dependency_NFE2L2, TP53 loss with weaker dependency_USP7, and PTEN loss with stronger dependency_STAT3. The remaining scientific question is whether these are independent effects, additive effects, redundant effects, or context-modified effects when central pathway lesions co-occur. I therefore register clean interaction tests and complementary conditional main effects for TP53/KEAP1 on USP7 and PTEN/TP53 on STAT3, using eligibility restrictions that exclude other central pathway contexts to make effect modification more interpretable. For USP7, positive signed effects indicate weaker USP7 dependency; for STAT3, negative raw effects or positive signed effects under direction -1 indicate stronger STAT3 dependency. I also register central context checks that were not fully covered: PTEN/STAT3 in SMARCA4-loss cells, TP53/USP7 in MSI-high cells, KEAP1/NFE2L2 in MSI-high cells, SMARCA4/SMARCA2 in MYC-amplified cells, and KEAP1/USP7 in TP53-loss cells. Finally, a specificity test asks whether KEAP1 loss has a clinically meaningful association with dependency_WRN, which would challenge WRN association specificity. All comparisons use the same pre-specified benchmark: valid Welch cells with at least 20 observations per cell; a signed 95% lower bound above +0.10 supports a clinically meaningful association or interaction in the registered direction; an interval excluding +0.10 on the favorable side supports rejection; intervals crossing +0.10 remain unresolved. These are within-dataset association/effect-modification hypotheses, not causal claims or claims about external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 18 explore. Current accepted within-dataset central associations: MSI_high -> stronger dependency_WRN; SMARCA4_loss -> stronger dependency_SMARCA2; KEAP1_loss -> weaker dependency_NFE2L2; TP53_loss -> weaker dependency_USP7; PTEN_loss -> stronger dependency_STAT3. Main effects have been broadly robust across library, batch, QC, lineage, mutation burden, PIK3CA, MYC, ARID1A, CDKN2A, KEAP1, TP53, STK11, MTAP, SMARCA4, and PTEN contexts. Therefore new exploration should prioritize targeted co-mutation decomposition, not broad additional main effects. Registering: (1) clean TP53 x KEAP1 interaction on USP7 with PTEN, SMARCA4, and MSI excluded, plus conditional KEAP1 and TP53 main effects in each central context; (2) clean PTEN x TP53 interaction on STAT3 with KEAP1, SMARCA4, and MSI excluded, plus PTEN main effects in TP53-loss and TP53-wt clean contexts; (3) remaining central context checks for PTEN/STAT3 in SMARCA4-loss, TP53/USP7 in MSI-high, KEAP1/NFE2L2 in MSI-high, SMARCA4/SMARCA2 in MYC amplification, and KEAP1/USP7 in TP53-loss; (4) specificity test KEAP1 loss on dependency_WRN. Interpretation benchmark remains signed lower bound > +0.10 for clinically meaningful positive support, upper bound < +0.10 for rejection, and crossing intervals unresolved. For dependency scores, direction -1 corresponds to more negative score/stronger dependency in exposed; direction +1 corresponds to less negative score/weaker dependency. No external generalizability or causality is claimed."
