  "narrative": "In this synthesis I assessed the new validation for H203, the PTEN-loss versus non-PTEN-loss contrast for dependency_STAT3 in cells with TP53 loss that also lack KEAP1 loss, SMARCA4 loss, and MSI-high. Because H203 has direction -1, a positive signed estimate means more negative dependency_STAT3 in PTEN-loss cells, i.e. stronger STAT3 dependency. The signed validation estimate is +0.206, direction-consistent with stronger STAT3 dependency, but the independent validation interval is -0.055 to +0.467. The lower bound is below zero and below the +0.10 clinically meaningful benchmark used throughout this run. The exposed cell contains only 30 samples, so the strict co-mutation context is underpowered. I therefore keep H203 unresolved and close further investigation: the data do not establish a clinically meaningful PTEN-loss effect in this narrow TP53-loss/non-KEAP1-loss/non-SMARCA4-loss/non-MSI-high context, but they also do not exclude a clinically meaningful positive effect.\n\nThis does not undermine the central PTEN-loss/dependency_STAT3 association. The broad PTEN-loss effect is accepted and independently validated, with signed estimates near +0.27 to +0.33 and validated lower bounds above +0.25 in H93, H102, and several stratum checks. H203 shows that the narrowest clean co-mutation context remains uncertain, while H204 supports a clinically meaningful PTEN-loss effect in TP53-wildtype clean contexts and H102 supports a meaningful effect among TP53-loss cells without the additional KEAP1/SMARCA4/MSI restrictions.\n\nFinal scientific report. Within this supplied lung cancer dependency dataset, five central measured associations are supported as scientifically meaningful under the pre-specified absolute dependency-score shift benchmark of +0.10 or greater. First, MSI-high status is associated with very strong dependency_WRN, signed effect approximately +0.79, with independent validation at +0.80 and an independent lower bound well above the benchmark. This is specific: BRCA2 loss does not show a clinically meaningful WRN effect among MSI-negative cells, and KEAP1 loss is not meaningfully associated with dependency_WRN. Second, SMARCA4 loss is associated with very strong dependency_SMARCA2, signed effect approximately +0.78, with independent validation and robustness across libraries, lineages, batches, QC subsets, mutation-burden bands, PIK3CA strata, TP53/CDKN2A/KEAP1/ARID1A/MYC contexts, and SMARCA4-loss contexts. ARID1A loss alone is not a meaningful predictor of dependency_SMARCA2 in SMARCA4-wildtype cells, supporting specificity. Third, KEAP1 loss is associated with very weak dependency_NFE2L2, signed effect approximately +0.80, with independent validation and robustness across TP53, PIK3CA, MYC, ARID1A, STK11, MSI, library, and QC contexts. STK11 loss alone is not a meaningful predictor of dependency_NFE2L2 in KEAP1-wildtype cells. Fourth, TP53 loss is associated with much weaker dependency_USP7, signed effect approximately +0.39, with independent validation and robustness across PIK3CA, CDKN2A, STK11, MTAP, KEAP1, ARID1A, MSI-negative, library, QC, and mutation-burden contexts; mutation-burden interaction did not survive validation. Fifth, PTEN loss is associated with stronger dependency_STAT3, signed effect approximately +0.30, with independent validation and robustness across PIK3CA, TP53, KRAS, MYC, KEAP1, ARID1A, MTAP, mutation-burden, QC, and co-mutation-restricted TP53-wildtype contexts.\n\nThe run also yields many important negative findings. The data do not support clinically meaningful oncotarget-concordant dependency for several canonical predictor-target pairs: KRAS mutation is not meaningfully associated with dependency_KRAS; BRAF V600E is not meaningfully associated with dependency_BRAF; EGFR amplification is not meaningfully associated with dependency_EGFR; ERBB2 amplification is not meaningfully associated with dependency_ERBB2. MYC amplification is not a meaningful predictor for most dependencies tested, including dependency_WRN, dependency_MDM2, dependency_POLR2A, dependency_RPL11, dependency_DHX9, dependency_TMED10, dependency_STAT3 in PTEN-wildtype cells, dependency_SMARCA2 in SMARCA4-wildtype cells, and dependency_USP7. ARID1A loss is not a meaningful independent predictor of dependency_SMARCA2 in SMARCA4-wildtype cells. STK11 loss is not meaningful for dependency_NFE2L2 in KEAP1-wildtype cells. MTAP loss is not meaningful for dependency_USP7 or dependency_MDM2 independently of TP53 loss. Research markers D, E, and F do not produce central clinically meaningful dependency effects. PTEN-loss interaction with TP53 loss for dependency_STAT3 was not favorable enough to support clinically meaningful interaction, and KEAP1 loss is not meaningfully associated with dependency_WRN, supporting WRN specificity for MSI-high status.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains mainly at the level of co-mutation decomposition and library/subgroup heterogeneity. Several interaction intervals for central pathway contexts are wide or do not cross the +0.10 benchmark, including PTEN-loss effects in the narrow TP53-loss/non-KEAP1-loss/non-SMARCA4-loss/non-MSI-high stratum, PTEN-loss effects in the KY CRISPR-library subgroup, KEAP1-loss effects on dependency_USP7 in TP53-loss cells, TP53-loss effects on dependency_USP7 in MSI-high cells, and several mutation-burden or ARID1A effect-modification checks. These unresolved items are not evidence of null; they mostly reflect small exposed cells and wide independent intervals. The central associations themselves are supported independently of these narrow subgroup tests. The conclusions are within-dataset associations. They do not establish causality, therapeutic sufficiency, or external generalizability.\n\nMost informative next analysis: stop expanding subgroup Welch comparisons. The most valuable next step is either external replication in an independent cohort or an adjusted joint model that simultaneously evaluates MSI-high, SMARCA4 loss, KEAP1 loss, TP53 loss, and PTEN loss, with appropriate interaction tests for co-occurring pathway lesions. If using only this dataset, the highest-yield targets would be pre-specified multivariable interaction models for PTEN loss x TP53 loss on dependency_STAT3 and TP53 loss x KEAP1 loss on dependency_USP7, because the main effects are robust but the co-occurrence effects are underpowered in single Welch subgroup contrasts.",
